a:257:{i:0;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"document_start";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:0;}i:1;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:0;}i:2;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:30:"Games Suspended Directors Play";}i:2;i:1;}i:3;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:31;}i:4;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:31;}i:5;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:450:"Suspended directors, particularly those whose interests may be at risk due to the insolvency process, sometimes attempt various tactics to delay or disrupt the corporate insolvency resolution process (CIRP). Their goal is often to regain control or, at the very least, influence the proceedings in a way that protects their interests. Here are some common strategies they might use to stall the resolution process:
Legal Appeals and Procedural Delays";}i:2;i:33;}i:6;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:483;}i:7;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"preformatted";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:494:" Tactics: Suspended directors may file multiple legal appeals and objections to procedural steps in the CIRP. This could involve challenging the admission of claims, disputing the appointment of the insolvency professional, or questioning the decisions of the creditors’ committee.
 Impact: These legal challenges often delay the CIRP as they require time for legal scrutiny and responses, slowing down crucial steps like claim verification, asset valuation, and approval of resolution plans.";}i:2;i:483;}i:8;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:483;}i:9;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:48:"Non-Cooperation with the Insolvency Professional";}i:2;i:983;}i:10;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1031;}i:11;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"preformatted";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:450:" Tactics: Suspended directors may withhold essential documents, financial statements, or operational information from the insolvency professional. They might delay responses to information requests or provide incomplete or misleading data.
 Impact: Non-cooperation can severely hinder the insolvency professional’s ability to assess the financial health of the company, prepare an accurate information memorandum, and conduct fair asset valuations.";}i:2;i:1031;}i:12;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1031;}i:13;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:31:"Asset Diversion and Concealment";}i:2;i:1487;}i:14;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1518;}i:15;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"preformatted";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:491:" Tactics: Suspended directors may try to conceal or divert assets, especially those not already disclosed or recorded, to reduce the asset base available for creditors. This might include transferring assets to related parties or claiming that certain assets are “disputed” or “under litigation.”
 Impact: Concealment and misreporting make asset valuation and recovery more challenging, reducing potential returns for creditors and prolonging the valuation and forensic audit stages.";}i:2;i:1518;}i:16;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1518;}i:17;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:46:"Fabricating Claims or Favoring Related Parties";}i:2;i:2015;}i:18;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2061;}i:19;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"preformatted";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:468:" Tactics: Directors might inflate claims from entities related to them or create fictitious creditors to influence the voting power within the creditors’ committee. By doing so, they can retain some control over decision-making or obstruct consensus on resolution plans.
 Impact: This tactic complicates the claim verification process, delays decision-making within the creditors’ committee, and often leads to further scrutiny, prolonging the resolution timeline.";}i:2;i:2061;}i:20;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2061;}i:21;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:43:"Influencing Potential Resolution Applicants";}i:2;i:2536;}i:22;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2579;}i:23;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"preformatted";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:540:" Tactics: Suspended directors may discourage potential resolution applicants from submitting viable plans by creating uncertainty about the company’s assets, ongoing litigations, or operational stability. They may also try to collaborate with certain applicants to create lowball offers that benefit the directors if the business is retained.
 Impact: This can reduce the pool of credible applicants, leading to inadequate resolution plans and potentially forcing creditors to reconsider liquidation, which can further extend the process.";}i:2;i:2579;}i:24;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:2579;}i:25;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:46:"Challenging the Valuation and Resolution Plans";}i:2;i:3125;}i:26;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3171;}i:27;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"preformatted";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:465:" Tactics: Suspended directors may dispute asset valuations or the feasibility of proposed resolution plans, claiming undervaluation or misrepresentation. They might also submit counterclaims to undermine the credibility of prospective applicants.
 Impact: Frequent challenges to valuations and plans lead to re-evaluations, additional reports, and delays in plan approval. This tactic is effective in stretching the process, especially if supported by legal action.";}i:2;i:3171;}i:28;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3171;}i:29;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:52:"Delaying Information Disclosure for Resolution Plans";}i:2;i:3642;}i:30;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:3694;}i:31;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"preformatted";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:422:" Tactics: Suspended directors can delay the release of critical information required for prospective applicants to make informed bids. They may also argue about confidentiality or contest the nature of disclosures required.
